A||||39||deceler8||Time for a devastating response...||||1||
Z||000000||deceler8||09-13-2001||11:04 AM||||I don't propose this lightly, but...<P>Terrorists crossed the line. John McCain said it best..."May God have mercy, because we won't"...<P>I believe our proper response to this crisis is the immediate destruction of all energy, communications, transportation and military installations in Afghanistan by the use of tactical nuclear weapons.<P>I'm not advocating the use of hydrogen bombs to wipe out Afghanistan...the judicious use of 1-2 kiloton tactical nukes aboard cruise missiles.<P>The primitive state of these installations would only require a limited strike to completely destroy these installations...perhaps no more than 3-5 weapons. The terrorists staged this attack in such a way that the defining images of this attack were the sights of our own aircraft destroying our own people and symbols.<P>I feel it is appropriate that our actions demonstrate our resolve and spread incredible fear and suffering to those that perpetrated it. Destruction of the above assets could plunge Afghanistan into darkness for decades and instantly and perhaps permanently isolate the nation.<P>The horror of war has been largely forgotten by America...so has the horror of nuclear weapons. The actions I have described above ironically could be done with a minimum of civilian casualties, which was not a consideration of the terrorists.<P>I also advocate the destruction of the Pakistani nuclear capability by whatever means necessary.<P>We must unleash a horror on the perpetrators that dwarfs what they unleashed on us. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cheever3000||09-13-2001||11:40 AM||||I was listening to the interview of Netanyahu, and his description of what it would take to defeat terrorism is more than what the U.S. would be willing to do.  It means the destruction of Iran, Iraq, Arafat, and possibly Afganistan & Pakistan.  That's when I realized our leaders are not up to doing the unpleasant things that must be done to keep this from happening again.  I also don't believe the rest of the world would LET us carry that out, even if we tried.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||deceler8||09-13-2001||11:54 AM||||My point is that we now fear attack...that fear of devastating retaliation must permeate all those who sponsor and provide assistance to these terrorist groups.<P>The horror of incredible sickness, destruction, starvation...the fear of a "living hell"...that is what has kept mankind from the brink and it is that fear that IMO will be most successful in preventing these kinds of future attacks.<P>We must also irrevocably demonstrate who our friends are and what behaviors we will not tolerate in any form. <P>The forces of good must destroy the forces of evil by whatever means necessary. <P>May God forgive us for our actions, whatever they may be.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fformula88||09-13-2001||12:56 PM||||I am not sure the use of force is necessarily needed for good to prevail here.  It is a touchy situation because blowing up too much... killing too many... defeats our own victory.<P>I am all for a military operation to bring those responsible to justice if thats what it takes.  However, the flexing of America's might for the sake of it isn't necessary.  The terrorists and the World know we can blow up Afghanistan, or the whole middle east for that matter.  <P>I think it would be a far greater victory for freedom and democracy to achieve our goals of finding justice, and getting the nations of the world to stop hosting and helping terrorism without actually blowing them up.<P>I heard a quote from someone (not exactly who)... it was "Lets bomb them back to the stone age!"  Well, what if they are already in the stone age?<P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero2m6siga.JPG">   <BR>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
