Z||000025||deceler8||09-13-2001||03:45 PM||||I agree...we have non-nuclear weapons in our arsenal that have the ability to produce ghastly results. And I'm sure there are weapons we possess that few, if any, realize we possess...<P>It's likely these weapons produce devastation that are more ghastly than any nuclear weapon...slow, painful deaths and horrible destruction. Many didn't realize we possessed stealth technology until the Gulf War, when in fact many of these planes were built in the 1970's.<P>I'm certain one of the revelations of this incident will in fact be the horrible, devastating weapons we use in reprisal that none of us knew existed.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Tonker||09-13-2001||04:01 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B> So, what about the nukes we used in Iraq during Desert Storm?  Small tactical nukes.  Not the big ones like Hiroshima or what most people think of when they think "nuke" but nuclear weapons, nontheless.  <P>Grow up and face reality.  A nuclear arsenal is there for a reason.  As a deterrant, and, if necessary, as a means of destruction.  I'm not advocating the use of nukes, or "glassing" a country.  I'm simply stating that to say, because they are nukes we shouldn't use them, is simplistic.<P>I hope it's not necessary to bomb a country back into the stone age, but if that's the course of action deemed necessary... so be it.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Nukes weren't used in Desert Storm as was previously mentioned.<P>Let me just clear it up for you:<BR>Nukes are <B>NOT</B> an acceptable solution in any situation, by any group or country. Nor are Biological or Chemical weapons. They are indiscriminate and irresponsible weapons.||||reg||1
