Z||000028||Taijiguy||09-13-2001||04:17 PM||||Just as a bit of education for most of you. The nuclear weapons made today are not dirty. Once the bomb has exploded, there is very little "fallout" left over. Without going into alot of technical details, I'll just say this: The bombs most people are familiar with were the ones used in Hiroshima and Nagasaki. Those were so large, they had to be assembled on board the bomber plane. They had a payload of about 10 megatons. Today, our high power bombs (one style) are about 6 feet tall, cone shaped, with a base of about 24". They have a payload of about 10x those first bombs. Approximately three days after detonation, no trace of radioactivity would exist. How do I know this? 8 years in the Navy on a submarine as a Ballistic Missile Technician. I maintained the Posiedon (C-3) missiles on board. I say let 'em fly. We need to prove a point.<BR>I think the very best method of locating bin-Laden would be to offer a HUGE reward for information on his whereabouts. Make it large enough, and even his most loyal followers would flip on him. We can spend millions upon millions and still not find him, I say just take the cash, and offer it up as a reward. 10, 20, 50 million is alot of motivation to turn someone over.||||reg||4
Z||000029||Tonker||09-13-2001||06:26 PM||||I gather they're already offering $5 mil for Bin-Ladin.||||reg||1
Z||000030||stsmithgt||09-13-2001||06:35 PM||||It's a bit old school, but I like those bombs that are filled with conductive carbon material.  Blast those over a power or communication station  or what ever and they'll have problems for a long time over and over.  Every time a good dust storm kicks up, it covers their equipment with that dust and will short them out again.  They'd have to keep cleaning and repairing.  They can't seal up everything and it's labor intensive.  On top of that, just blow the holy hell out of them too.  Him, his friends, associates, aquantances, fan club, and all the others that even gave him a smile including his MOTHER, she doesn't need to breed anymore.<P>Yeah I'm pissed,  I have a flight next saterday on American Airlines and don't like the idea Laden likes using them as darts.  The airline probably won't let me take my guns with me to go hunting back in Arkansas now too.  That really steams me up.  Now I need to figure another way to relax on this <I>WAY</I> overdue vacation. !#@$@$%#@$%&^$#@&^  BLOOD PRESSURE! BLOOD PRESSURE!!!! I have an itchy trigger finger for a certain ragtop and I don't mean convertible.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> Bring him on!  I'll take pictures too.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Bin_laden.jpg">   <P>------------------<BR>My work of art.(in progress.)      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carpicsig.jpg">    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/coltlogo.gif"> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by stsmithgt (edited 09-13-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000031||Sootah||09-13-2001||06:50 PM||||Please splain more about these 'clean' nukes you speak of. I like em. Ya know what, after watching the actual families that are torn up about this; Knowing that there are thousands more families feeling the same or worse.. I say we nuke em till they are nothing but a hole in the ground. The only thing that I am still worried about is a retaliatory nuclear attack. I'm not in an area that would be hit, but I'm sure that their nukes arent as environmentally friendly as ours......<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif">   <BR><I>Never pull your pants down in Cosco, the dang monkeys will getcha</I> <BR><B>AIM</B>: KSSouter||||reg||4
Z||000032||LZeitgeist||09-13-2001||06:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Taijiguy sez:<BR>Just as a bit of education for most of you. The nuclear weapons made today are not dirty. Once the bomb has exploded, there is very little "fallout" left over. Without going into alot of technical details, I'll just say this: The bombs most people are familiar with were the ones used in Hiroshima and Nagasaki. Those were so large, they had to be assembled on board the bomber plane. They had a payload of about 10 megatons. Today, our high power bombs (one style) are about 6 feet tall, cone shaped, with a base of about 24". They have a payload of about 10x those first bombs. Approximately three days after detonation, no trace of radioactivity would exist. <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I believe you 110%.. I'm definitely not going to call BS on *you*, my friend. &lt;g&gt;<P>However, the part I don't understand is how do they "adjust" the half-life of the radioactivity to only last three days? Isn't something that's radioactive that way for a *really* long time, or else it's not radioactive enough to do any damage to anything around it?<P>I'm thinking this isn't classified stuff, since you just posted it for the world to see, but I'd never heard of this stuff before... can you explain just a bit more?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000033||Taijiguy||09-13-2001||07:12 PM||||Frankly, it's been so long, I don't really recall the mechanics of it. But essentially, much less raw material is necessary to produce a much hotter explosion. Additionally, the explosive used to initiate the reaction is much more precise, as well as the detonation methods. On the bombs I was describing, the actual explosive and raw materials were no larger than a softball. Basically when you have so much less raw material to begin with, it's scattered farther and less densely. The result is a residual radioactivity level about the same as the luminescent numbers on your watch.||||reg||1
Z||000034||Mach10||09-13-2001||07:23 PM||||Tajiguy: You would be refering to Fusion bombs (aka Hydrogen bombs) Those use a radioactive isotope of Hydrogen. They have a larger payload, and DO have less fallout. Halflife of the residual Deutrium (very bad stuff) is about 1.5 days. The fusion bomb actually CREATES radioactive elements due to the massive amount of energy release. <BR>I was under the impression that the Hiroshima/Nagasaki bombs were in the 10-20 Kiloton range. Current US tested bombs haven't gone past 40 Megatons, so I thought. I bel1ieve the Soviets have the most powerful bomb tested, a 60 Megaton FFF bomb. VERY powerful, VERY destructive, VERY dirty. It's a 3-stage weapon, Fission reaction sets off a Fusion charge, AND another Fission charge. Radioactive Cs spread all over the place with half-lives in the 100 year range.<P>I will agree with the previous statement. There is NO acceptable use of weapons of mass-destruction. ||||reg||3
Z||000035||87GTBro||09-13-2001||07:55 PM||||The absolute destruction of all those countries will not even start to get rid of the terrorist activities.  In fact, it would strengthen it.  <P>The reason these people become terrorists is because of lies their leaders and governments have told them about the United States.<P>If we go over there and start bombing all those countries, it would only turn the lies these people have been told into truth.  That "the United States is evil and trying to take over the world."<P>Many of you saw those kids in the streets in Afghanistan celebrating and cheering and singing "God has brought judgement on the United States!"  Where do you think these kids heard the United States was evil?  Probably from their teachers and parents and leaders in the country.<P>Bombing these countries will only strengthen their beliefs and possibly cause others to believe.  Kids grow up.  The men who flew those planes were kids at one time.  Destruction is not the solution.  It would just cause a deaper hatred to swell up inside more people.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/87GTBrosig_3.gif">   <BR>AOL IM: GTFiero86Dan<BR>'86 GT 4 speed<BR>'87 GT Auto<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTBro (edited 09-13-2001).]||||reg||1
